  "result": "Not quite. A model doesn't remember anything on its own. It only sees the text you send it on each call.\n\nSay Sam tells the assistant on Monday, \"I finished the laser cutter training.\" On Friday Sam starts a new session and asks, \"Can I book the laser cutter?\" The model gets only that Friday question, so it has no idea about Monday. To make it \"remember\", your program has to do three things:\n\n1. Save a note, like `{\"sam\": [\"laser cutter training done\"]}`, in a file or database.\n2. Load that note at the start of Friday's session.\n3. Paste it into the text the model gets, e.g. \"Known about this student: laser cutter training done.\"\n\nThen the model can answer as if it remembered. This ties straight back to what you already worked out: the model only knows its training plus what's in its input.\n\nThis matters for your design review. \"What gets saved between sessions\" is a decision your team has to make and build. It won't happen by default, and whatever you paste back in also costs tokens.\n\nQuick check: if Sam's training status changes on Wednesday, what would your program have to do so Friday's session isn't working from stale info?",
